AI Agents (Beyond Standard Generation)
Standard AI models are reactive; they answer a single prompt and stop. AI Agents are autonomous systems designed to execute complex, multi-step objectives over time.
 Tool Integration: Agents can actively interface with external environments, executing Python scripts, querying databases, or calling web APIs to gather live data.
 Reasoning Engine: They possess the ability to break down a massive goal (like migrating a pipeline) into smaller, logical sub-tasks.
 Contextual Memory: Agents maintain awareness of previous steps, allowing them to adapt their strategy if a specific approach fails mid-execution.
Agentic Workflows (The Iterative Loop)
An agentic workflow is the structured framework in which an AI Agent operates. It replaces the traditional "zero-shot" prompt with a continuous feedback loop.
 The Four-Step Cycle: The workflow generally consists of Planning (defining the architecture), Execution (writing the code), Reflection (running a test), and Correction (fixing any errors).
 Self-Correction: If the AI writes a PySpark script that throws a syntax error, the workflow allows it to read the error log and rewrite the code before presenting it to the user.
 Quality Assurance: This iterative looping drastically reduces AI hallucinations and ensures the final output is much closer to production-ready.
Model Context Protocol (MCP)
MCP is an open standard that acts as a secure, universal bridge between AI models and your local enterprise data.
 Context Automation: It eliminates the need for developers to manually copy and paste code into a chat window by allowing the AI to directly view local files, databases, and APIs.
 Secure Integration: It ensures that the AI can read your proprietary Databricks or Synapse configurations without exporting sensitive company data to public AI servers.
 Standardized Access: MCP provides a uniform way to connect any supported AI model to your local engineering workspace seamlessly.
Claude Code & CLI Tools
Tools like Claude Code represent the next evolution of developer assistance by putting the AI directly inside the developer's command-line interface (CLI).
 Native Environment: Instead of working in a separate browser tab, the AI lives where the engineer works, possessing full visibility of the local codebase and folder structures.
 Execution Capabilities: These CLI agents can independently run bash commands, execute unit tests, and even stage files for Git commits.
 Workflow Acceleration: It allows an engineer to type a command like "refactor this Synapse logic into PySpark," and the tool will create, write, and save the actual ⁠.py⁠ file directly onto the machine.
